  "analysis": "We need to determine the minimum number of insertions of the letter 'x' to turn a given string s into a palindrome, where only 'x' can be inserted. A two-pointer approach works: use i at the start and j at the end. While i < j, if s[i] == s[j], move both ends inward. If they differ, we can only fix by inserting an 'x' to pair with an 'x' on one side: if s[i] == 'x', skip i and count one insertion; if s[j] == 'x', skip j and count one insertion. If neither end is 'x' and they differ, it is impossible (-1). This yields the minimal number of insertions because every insertion corresponds to eliminating an unmatched 'x' at one end. Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) extra space.",
  "plan": "1) Implement a small, fast program (C++ is robust here) to compute the minimal insertions using the two-pointer approach described above. 2) Compile the program. 3) Test it against the provided samples to verify correctness. 4) Provide the final solution behavior on additional edge cases (e.g., all characters same, large inputs).",
